          "heading": "why_in_hospital",
          "source_ids": [
            "sections.0",
            "sections.1"
          ],
          "text": "You came in while in labour. Your baby's heart rate showed signs of distress, so you needed an emergency caesarean section (LSCS) to deliver your baby safely. You were 39 weeks pregnant.\n\nAfter the birth you had heavy bleeding (postpartum haemorrhage). This was because your womb did not contract as it should."
        },
        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.1",
          "heading": "what_we_did",
          "source_ids": [
            "sections.2",
            "sections.3"
          ],
          "text": "To stop the bleeding, the team gave you medicines called oxytocin and ergometrine, and used a technique called bimanual compression. This worked well and you did not need to go back to theatre.\n\nYour blood count was lower after the birth than before. Because of this, the team started you on iron tablets to help your blood recover.\n\nBy day 2 you were up and moving around. Feeding was going well, your wound was clean, and your baby was doing well."
        },
        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.2",
          "heading": "medicines",
          "source_ids": [],
          "text": "{{MEDICATIONS}}"
        },
        {
